<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Voting Rights Act of 1965</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Voting Rights Act of 1965 is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> law, signed by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyndon-b-johnson">Lyndon B. Johnson</a> in August 1965 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches">Selma to Montgomery marches</a>, that prohibited racial discrimination in voting. It suspended literacy tests and placed federal oversight on the states and counties with histories of discrimination, leading to a dramatic rise in Black voter registration in the South; a nationwide ban on such tests followed in 1970.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965.jpg" alt="Voting Rights Act of 1965" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yoichi Okamoto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyndon_Johnson_and_Martin_Luther_King,_Jr._-_Voting_Rights_Act.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifteenth-amendment">Fifteenth Amendment</a> (1870) barred denial of the vote on account of race, color or previous condition of servitude and empowered Congress to enforce it. Congress passed the Enforcement Acts in the 1870s, criminalizing obstruction of voting and providing federal supervision of elections, but in 1875 the Supreme Court struck down parts of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> v. Cruikshank and United States v. Reese. After 1877 enforcement became erratic, and in 1894 Congress repealed most of the provisions.</p><p>Case-by-case litigation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Justice had failed to overcome the resistance of state officials, and Congress concluded that a comprehensive new law was needed. The bill was introduced on 17 March 1965 as S. 1964 by the Senate majority leader Mike Mansfield and minority leader Everett Dirksen, who had drafted it with Attorney General Katzenbach. Dirksen agreed to back it after learning of the police violence against marchers at Selma on Bloody Sunday.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Section 2 prohibits any state or local government from imposing voting rules that restrict the vote on account of race, color or membership in a language minority group. The act also outlaws literacy tests and similar devices. Its special provisions apply to jurisdictions with a history of discrimination, chiefly through the Section 5 requirement that they obtain approval from the attorney general or a federal court before changing any voting rule.</p><p>The Senate passed the bill on 26 May 1965, the Republican Party voting 30 to 2 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> 47 to 17. The House of Representatives followed on 9 July, the Republican Party voting 112 to 23 and the Democratic Party 221 to 62. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyndon-b-johnson">Lyndon B. Johnson</a> signed it on 6 August 1965.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The effect was immediate. By the end of 1965 a quarter of a million new Black voters had been registered, a third of them by federal examiners. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> the share of adult <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> registered rose from 19.3 percent in 1964 to 61.3 percent in 1969, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> from 6.7 percent to 66.5 percent.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> v. Katzenbach, decided on 7 March 1966, the Supreme Court held the act a constitutional means of enforcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifteenth-amendment">Fifteenth Amendment</a>. Congress amended it in 1970, 1975, 1982, 1992 and 2006, extending the special provisions each time one of them was due to expire, and in 1970 it created a nationwide ban on literacy tests. In Shelby County v. Holder (2013) the Court nullified the preclearance requirement for the covered jurisdictions, reasoning that it was obsolete.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Voting Rights Act of 1965” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1877 Democratic legislatures excluded most Black people and many poor whites from the vote until the <b>Voting Rights Act of 1965</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The civil rights movement, with leaders such as Martin Luther King Jr., won the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the <b>Voting Rights Act of 1965</b>, ending legal segregation and restoring Black voting rights in the South.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The march to Montgomery was completed under federal protection on 25 March, and on 6 August Johnson signed the <b>Voting Rights Act of 1965</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In the American South, poll taxes, literacy tests and intimidation kept most Black women, like Black men, from voting until the <b>Voting Rights Act of 1965</b>, and Asian immigrants were barred from citizenship, and so from voting, until the 1940s and 1950s.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Their deaths spurred support for the <b>Voting Rights Act of 1965</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-21">21 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
